Release signing keys
====================

This directory holds the OpenPGP public keys whose signatures the node scripts
accept on a telcoin-network release and on its source tag. setup-node.sh
(--install-method release, and Docker installs given a release tag),
update-node.sh and edit-config.sh (a Docker image given as a release tag)
download a release's SHA256SUMS and SHA256SUMS.asc and run

    gpgv --status-fd 1 --keyring <every *.asc here, dearmored> SHA256SUMS.asc SHA256SUMS

and read its status lines:

  - accepted only when at least one signature is good (GOODSIG then VALIDSIG)
    and its primary key fingerprint belongs to one of these files;
  - refused on any BADSIG, by whichever key;
  - refused on EXPKEYSIG, REVKEYSIG or EXPSIG: these come only from a key in
    the keyring, which holds the keys listed here, so an expired or revoked
    listed key refuses the release even beside another good signature;
  - refused when no listed key made a good signature: an unsigned file, or one
    signed only by keys that are not here;
  - a further signature by a key that is not here (NO_PUBKEY, or ERRSIG for a
    missing key) is not checked and does not refuse: the scripts print a
    warning that names its key id, once. Any other ERRSIG refuses.

Why an unknown co-signer only warns: the release pipelines refuse to sign,
verify or publish a SHA256SUMS.asc that carries any signature that is not
good or not by an allowlisted maintainer key, and the manual check on the
install page refuses one too. So a published release only ever carries
allowlisted signatures, and the warning covers the one case left: a co-signer
allowlisted upstream whose key has not shipped here yet (this directory lags
tn-release-signing until a script release moves its submodule pointer, see
"Adding or removing a key" below).

Then every file used from the release (the tarball, IMAGE_DIGEST) must match its
SHA256SUMS line, and the chain-config lines must carry the hashes these scripts
pin. IMAGE_DIGEST must name an image repository a new pull for the network
takes (on mainnet ghcr.io/telcoin-association/tn-mainnet; on testnet
ghcr.io/telcoin-association/tn-testnet or the Artifact Registry adiri
repository). The old single ghcr.io package, which never held a release, is
refused. Nothing from a release that fails a check is installed or run.

Source builds check the release tag with the same keys: git verify-tag runs in
a throwaway GnuPG home that holds them. A bad, expired or revoked tag signature
refuses the build on every network. A tag that cannot be checked (no key here,
gpg missing, an unsigned tag, or a signer whose key is not here) refuses the
build on mainnet and builds with a warning on testnet.

firewall-setup.sh --p2p-committee on checks the tn-p2p-allow-sync release it
installs the same way: its SHA256SUMS.asc against these keys, then the
tarball against its SHA256SUMS line and the sha256 lib/common.sh pins.

Script releases
---------------

The same maintainer key signs node releases and script releases: these keys
sign the script releases of this repository too, from scripts-v2.1.0, the
first signed one. A release's MANIFEST.asc holds detached OpenPGP signatures
over its MANIFEST, and
update-scripts.sh and install.sh (whose embedded copy of these files is its
trust root) run gpgv --status-fd 1 against a keyring made from these files
alone. The release is accepted when at least TN_SIG_THRESHOLD of these keys
(one today) made a good signature and no signature is bad, expired or revoked;
the same status rules as above apply. These files are listed in the signed
MANIFEST like any script, so a script release that adds or removes a key must
be signed by a key the host already has. When no key is in both the old and
the new set, as in the scripts-v2.2.0 rotation from grantkee's RSA key to the
ed25519 keys, a host that holds only the old keys refuses the release ("not
signed by a Telcoin release key") and re-runs the installer, whose embedded
keys are the new ones. tools/release-scripts.sh prepares and checks a release;
tools/cut-release.sh runs the release steps.

Format
------

ASCII-armored public keys, one file per maintainer, named <github-handle>.asc.
They are byte-for-byte copies of keys/*.asc in the tn-release-signing
repository (https://github.com/Telcoin-Association/tn-release-signing), the
maintainers' key allowlist, at the commit tn-node-deployment records for its
submodule at vendor/tn-release-signing: the same set of names and the same
bytes. tools/check-release-keys.sh fails when they differ, and CI runs it.
The submodule is where the files come from, not where hosts read them: the
installer downloads GitHub's archive of the release tag, which never holds a
submodule's files, and the updater installs the files the signed MANIFEST
lists, so the keys ship here as ordinary files. Every *.asc file here is
read; other files (this README) are ignored. A file may carry more than one
primary key: every primary key (every "pub" that gpg --show-keys lists) in
every file joins the keyring and the fingerprint allowlist, whether the file
holds one armored block or several. A key that is expired or revoked stays
refused even while its file is here.

Shipped keys
------------

    handle      primary key fingerprint                    expires      added
    grantkee    95A38484E1CBD31D773F6AF928FD56AD2F9FAE47   2028-11-16   2026-11-17
    MavenRain   32F886ADE9C3A88218E4B67CA0A69159FC76DBFD   2028-11-10   2026-11-17

Both are ed25519 primary keys that sign, each on its holder's YubiKey; their
subkeys are not used here. The table in KEYS.md of tn-release-signing lists
the same fingerprints. grantkee.asc held grantkee's RSA-2048 key
4E36D35A4AB61B589861D01AC7173F2377CA7573 up to scripts-v2.1.0; that key was
replaced by the ed25519 one and removed from tn-release-signing, and it signs
nothing these scripts accept from scripts-v2.2.0 on.

Before a key expires, its maintainer extends the expiry, re-exports the public
key into tn-release-signing and ships the new file in a script release, the
same way a key is added below. Hosts must install that script release before
the old expiry date: from that date gpgv reports EXPKEYSIG for every release
signed with the key, and the scripts refuse those releases, script releases
included. Only a script release that verifies against the copy a host already
has can renew it, so a host that misses the window re-runs the installer,
whose embedded keys are the renewed ones. grantkee.asc expires 2028-11-16 and
MavenRain.asc 2028-11-10.

Adding or removing a key
------------------------

1. The change lands in tn-release-signing first: keys/<handle>.asc and its
   row in KEYS.md, reviewed there (its KEYS.md and docs/signing.md say how).

2. Move the submodule pointer to that commit, on a branch of
   tn-node-deployment that starts at an up-to-date main, and read what the
   move brings in:

       git submodule update --init -- vendor/tn-release-signing
       git -C vendor/tn-release-signing fetch --quiet origin main
       git -C vendor/tn-release-signing checkout --quiet --detach FETCH_HEAD
       git diff --submodule=log -- vendor/tn-release-signing
       git -C vendor/tn-release-signing diff "$(git rev-parse HEAD:vendor/tn-release-signing)" HEAD -- keys KEYS.md
       git add vendor/tn-release-signing

   Check every fingerprint against the KEYS.md table and against
   https://github.com/<handle>.gpg:

       gpg --show-keys --with-fingerprint vendor/tn-release-signing/keys/*.asc

3. Copy the files: lib/release-keys/*.asc become the keys/*.asc of the
   commit the pointer now records, the same names and the same bytes, taken
   from the submodule's object store:

       sha="$(git rev-parse :vendor/tn-release-signing)"
       rm -f lib/release-keys/*.asc lib/release-keys/*.asc.sha256
       git -C vendor/tn-release-signing ls-tree --name-only "$sha" keys/ |
           grep '\.asc$' | while IFS= read -r k; do
               git -C vendor/tn-release-signing cat-file blob "$sha:$k" > "lib/release-keys/${k#keys/}"
           done

   List every key file in update-scripts.sh SCRIPTS
   ("lib/release-keys/<handle>.asc:lib/release-keys/<handle>.asc:NONE") so
   the updater ships it, and drop the row of a key that is gone. Write the
   key files, in name order, into the TN_RELEASE_KEYS block of install.sh
   (tools/release-scripts.sh does it the same way when it prepares the
   release; tools/tests/install_test.sh checks that the two match).

4. Run the check, then refresh the sidecars:

       bash tools/check-release-keys.sh
       bash tools/gen-checksums.sh

   The check exits 0 when the copies match, 1 with every difference named
   when they do not, and 2 when it cannot read the submodule (run the
   git submodule update --init line of step 2). Commit the pointer and
   lib/release-keys together (git add -A lib/release-keys).

5. Publish a signed script release. Hosts pick the key set up with
   update-scripts.sh; the keys are part of the signed MANIFEST, like the
   scripts that read them. The Node Manager UI runs its own copy of the
   scripts and keys (/opt/telcoin-ui-update), which takes the new key set
   only with the next install-ui.sh --update, as for every other script file.

A release signed only by a key that is not here yet is refused until the
script release that carries the key is installed. A release whose
SHA256SUMS.asc carries a good signature by a key here and also a signature by
a key that is not here is taken on the first, with a warning that names the
second key's id (see above); ship every signer's key so the warning goes away.

While this directory holds no *.asc file, no release can be verified:
--install-method release, release updates and every mainnet image are
refused, and so are mainnet source builds. On testnet, a Docker install,
update or image change given a release tag then uses the digest the registry
serves for that tag, with a warning that no signed release confirms it (as it
does for a release that publishes no signed files), and a source build goes
ahead with a warning. A release whose files are published but fail a check is
never replaced by the registry's answer.
